    // The number of pairs with one left and one right endpoint:
    //
    // Let X be the number of crossing pairs.
    // The probability that a specific pair is crossing is:
    // P(a specific pair has one left and one right) = 2 * j * (2n-j) / ((2n)(2n-1))
    // But the pairs are not independent.
    //
    // Actually, the distribution of the number of crossing pairs when we randomly pair 2n items, 
    // j of which are "left" and 2n-j are "right", is well-known.
    //
    // The number of ways to pair 2n items is (2n-1)!! = (2n)!/(2^n n!).
    //
    // The number of pairings with exactly m crossing pairs:
    // We need to choose which m left endpoints pair with right endpoints, and which m right endpoints pair with left endpoints.
    // The remaining j-m left endpoints pair among themselves, and the remaining 2n-j-m right endpoints pair among themselves.
    //
    // Number of ways:
    // - Choose m left endpoints out of j: C(j, m)
    // - Choose m right endpoints out of 2n-j: C(2n-j, m)
    // - Pair the m chosen left with the m chosen right: m! ways
    // - Pair the remaining j-m left endpoints: (j-m-1)!! if j-m is even, 0 otherwise
    // - Pair the remaining 2n-j-m right endpoints: (2n-j-m-1)!! if 2n-j-m is even, 0 otherwise
    //
    // So the probability that exactly m segments cross is:
    // P(X=m) = [C(j,m) * C(2n-j,m) * m! * (j-m-1)!! * (2n-j-m-1)!!] / (2n-1)!!
    // where (-1)!! = 1 and (2k-1)!! = (2k)!/(2^k k!)
    //
    // Note: (j-m) must be even and (2n-j-m) must be even for the double factorials to be non-zero.
    // Since j + (2n-j) = 2n is even, j-m and 2n-j-m have the same parity. So we just need j-m to be even.
    //
